        "outcome": "The fused softmax kernel configuration is accepted and confirmed with a significant speedup (~1.67x) over the incumbent separate-kernel baseline. Fusion reduces kernel launch overhead and intermediate memory traffic, the main bottlenecks for this workload. The block sizes (block_m=16, block_n=16) effectively reduce masking overhead caused by irregular matrix dimensions (513x781). The choice of num_warps=4 and num_stages=2 balances resource usage and latency hiding without spills or occupancy issues. Tensor cores remain disabled due to float32 datatype and baseline usage. The fused kernel source implements stable numerics by subtracting the row max before exponentiation and correctly normalizing. No hardware bottlenecks or register spills were observed. Logical minimum traffic is improved, though direct DRAM traffic measurements are absent. The confirmed speedup demonstrates that fusion and launch reduction are critical for performance here. Next directions include: 1) exploring slightly larger block sizes (e.g., 32x32) to test tradeoffs between masking overhead and parallelism; 2) investigating enabling tensor cores if datatype changes to float16 or bfloat16; 3) analyzing memory coalescing and possibly adjusting layout or thread mapping to improve DRAM efficiency; 4) considering kernel batching or aggregation if launch overhead persists for small tiles; 5) profiling with hardware counters when available to identify further bottlenecks; 6) validating performance on other shapes to ensure generality of fusion benefits. Overall, the current fused kernel configuration is a strong improvement and a good baseline for further tuning and extensions.",
